{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the probability of obtaining a favorable outcome, where 'favorable' is not explicitly defined but implies a positive monetary outcome (i.e., any non-zero result). The differences between outcomes are considered negligible, so the specific amounts (e.g., 6000 vs. 4000 vs. 2000) are not decisive—only the likelihood of getting any positive payoff matters. For Lottery A: probability of a favorable outcome = 25% (only 6000 Euros with 25%). For Lottery B: probability of a favorable outcome = 50% (25% for 4000 + 25% for 2000). Thus, Lottery B has a higher probability of a favorable outcome and would be preferred by this reason.",
